Confirmed thrash: ino=14680194 (`uv_verify` barrier dir), incore_ftype=1 (file) vs dirent_ftype=2 (dir), firing 125× (~1/sec = once per barrier_wait poll). The in-place reload is **called but not sticking** — the type never flips. Either the reload's typeflip guard rejects it (disk_gen ≤ incore_gen) or disk still has the stale file (creator didn't flush). Let me check the reload skip reason and whether uv_delete passed this time.
**Root cause of the thrash:** `RELOAD-TYPEFLIP-STALE-SKIP ino=14680194 incore_mode=0100644(file) disk_mode=040755(dir) incore_gen=3551188422 disk_gen=1573697881` — the reload's typeflip guard rejects the flip because `disk_gen < incore_gen`. But the **dirent says DIR** (ftype=2) and **disk says DIR** — they agree, so this is a genuine reuse. The guard's assumption (newer incarnation always has higher gen) is **false because XFS generations are random**, not monotonic. So node1 keeps its stale file inode → can't use `uv_verify` as a dir → "Not a directory" → barrier times out.

The fix: the **dirent ftype is ground truth**. When the reload is triggered by a dirent/disk type that AGREE, bypass the gen-based stale guard. Let me thread `dirent_ftype` into `mxfs_dlm_reload_inode`.
